  <h2 id="verdict">Is YouTube a good career? Career or lottery: the short answer</h2>

  <p>
    YouTube is a platform, not a job. It pays some people well, a few people very well, and most people nothing. So the useful question is not "is YouTube good". It is "what will this channel sell, to whom, and what happens to my money while I find out".
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">It behaves like a career when</div>
      <ul>
        <li>The channel proves a skill and brings in clients, students or sponsors.</li>
        <li>You earn from more than one source, and at least one source is something you own.</li>
        <li>You are building a topic edge and a body of work, not chasing trends.</li>
        <li>Your living costs are covered by something else while it grows.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">It behaves like a lottery when</div>
      <ul>
        <li>The plan is "get views, then ads will pay".</li>
        <li>You copy what already went viral.</li>
        <li>You expect a date by which it will start paying.</li>
        <li>You leave study or a job for it on a few good videos.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The people you see winning are the survivors. For each one there are many channels that went quiet after a few uploads. Their stories are not a forecast for you. Their habits are worth copying: one clear topic, steady uploads and something to sell. Their luck is not.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="odds">What the numbers say about your odds</h2>

  <p>
    No one publishes a clean count of how many Indians try YouTube and how many earn. What exists are four partial views. Each counts something different, so do not divide one by another.
  </p>

