  <h2 id="faceless">Faceless and AI-made channels: what YouTube's policy says</h2>

  <p>
    A faceless channel is fine. A mass-produced one is not. YouTube's monetisation policy was clarified on 15 July 2025 to say that inauthentic content includes content that is repetitive or mass-produced, and it says reused content stays a problem even with the original maker's permission.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Not allowed:</strong> AI content made from generic or unoriginal templates that gives the impression of mass production, and AI personas posing as experts on sensitive topics, such as AI hosts giving financial advice.</li>
    <li><strong>Allowed:</strong> using AI as a creative tool, for example to edit scripts or to picture a character and story you invented.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Separately, YouTube asks you to label realistic AI content. A label alone does not limit your audience or your earning, but repeated failure to label can bring penalties, including removal from the Partner Programme.
  </p>

