    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    Count money the same way a small business does. Add the yearly cost of tools, music licences, storage, internet and any editor you hire. Subtract it from what the channel and its offer actually bring in, after tax and the share YouTube keeps.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you cannot see what pays for the next tool, wait before you buy it. Keep business and personal money apart from the first payment.
  </p>

  <p>
    A team usually starts with one part-time editor, then a thumbnail designer or script helper, as revenue allows. That is also a job market: the people you hire are doing the video work route above.
  </p>

