  <p>
    Skipping it as a full career does not mean skipping it. Many people keep a channel as a portfolio and lead source next to a job or degree, and decide later.
  </p>

  <p>
    College is a small part of this. Spend about 10% of your education budget on any course, and put the rest into tools, practice and living costs. Free videos and YouTube's own help pages teach the basics.
  </p>

  <p>
    Above roughly 20% of your budget, ask for course-specific proof. Before paying for any YouTube course, check whether the teacher's own channel shows results you can verify, what you will publish by the end, and the refund terms in writing. A pile of certificates is not a skill portfolio.
  </p>

